      "value": "@{5f2d86888fc2c972dd486dc6|full_name}: Hello! Thanks for getting back to me! I just went in and got those settings. I also tested it on both legacy and non-legacy while I was in there (even rejoining the instance between modes just to ensure everything was \"refreshed\"). Excuse the quality of the screenshot. 😅\n\nAlso, I have my logs for this session, if you'd like me to attach them or send them in via a ticket. I recall trying to add a scaling animation to my model over a year ago and the issue being the view position, so that's naturally what I navigated to while reviewing the logs. I feel that may be the issue in this update. See two lines from my log below:\n\n2023.08.03 21:55:25 Log        -  [VRCTrackingManager] [IK Debug Log] Changed player height... tracking scale:0.2291507 eye height:1.622014\n\n2023.08.03 21:55:31 Log        -  [VRCTrackingManager] [IK Debug Log] Changed player height... tracking scale:0.1125576 eye height:1.622014\n\nSorry if it's hard to read in this enclosed space, but note that the tracking scale of line 1 is 0.23, whereas the tracking scale of line 2 is 0.11. Despite this obvious difference, the eye height is \"capped\" at 1.62, refusing to go lower. My assumption is that this new setting tied the view position to the head bone or similar, but has a limited minimum Y value. If this is the case and the view position takes priority over the bone under this circumstance, the stretched neck and torso would be explained. This would also explain why the models seem to \"normalize\" as they grow taller and the proportions begin to equal out. I don't know how this would fit into the legs being bent other than the height calculation is obviously thrown off during recalibration if a bone is required to be at Y=1.622014 (see below).\n\n2023.08.03 21:55:31 Log        -  Measure Human Avatar Avatar isRemeasure:True\n\nI hope that this helps and gets some questions answered! My ideas are just a shot in the dark, but those eye height values from my log definitely raised an eyebrow from me. If you need anything further, including my logs, just let me know!",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2023-08-01T03:04:23.714Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "deletedBy": null,
  "details": "I don't believe this patch was tested using smaller models (<1 meter). My calibration on my model (0.41 meters) is scuffed. Knees bent, neck extended like a giraffe. This is attributed to the last patch (likely the avatar scaling update in particular). Until this is resolved, please let us either play on the last patch or hotfix the issue. FBT only begins to normalize at around 1 meter height, regardless of the < 1 meter starting height. \n\nPlease note, this issue persists without ever even messing with the new scaling features. You can probably begin diagnostics by looking at the scaling changes which affect models **before** applying the height scale.\n\n(Please don't hit me back with, \"Did you check your height setting in game?\" Everything is set up as it should be, and it was functioning perfectly prior to 2023.2.4. Multiple models tested, all with different rigs which worked perfectly prior to this build.)\n\nEDIT: Tested this in numerous models (more than just my own to include default VRC models) then went around a few public lobbies and asked other FBT users to shrink down. The same issues persisted amongst everybody. As it stands currently, the only way to have an enjoyable experience as a smaller model (regardless of whether you are using the new scale setting) is if you are not using FBT. Hope this gets fixed soon! I was pretty thorough, but lemme know if you need any more details and I'll set back up and get 'em for you!",
